<h2>Designing for hard water</h2>
<p>Hard water is the single most local problem in this corridor's kitchens. Calcium and magnesium dry into white rings and crusts on every surface that stays wet. Plan for it in five places:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>The sink.</strong> Steel 304 in a satin or matte finish, or a dark quartz (granite composite) sink. Avoid mirror-finish steel; every drop shows.</li>
  <li><strong>The countertop.</strong> Quartz is non-porous and wipes clean. Dark granite is cheaper and fine if sealed after fixing and every year after. Light, polished granite near the sink will mark.</li>
  <li><strong>The backsplash.</strong> Matte ceramic or quartz behind the sink. Lacquered glass looks smart but needs daily wiping where water splashes.</li>
  <li><strong>The tap.</strong> A tap with a removable aerator, so it can be soaked in vinegar and descaled. A pull-out spray helps keep the sink rinsed.</li>
  <li><strong>The services.</strong> A socket and a water point for an RO purifier, and if possible a softener on the inlet for the whole kitchen.</li>
</ol>
<?= img('stainless-steel-sink-and-quartz-countertop', caption: 'A satin steel 304 sink set in a dark quartz countertop: the combination that hides hard-water marks best') ?>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Tip</span>Seal granite before the first use, not after the first stain. Ask the installer to apply a penetrating stone sealer on the day the counter is fixed, and reseal once a year.</div>

